int clusterLockConfig(char *filename) {
    int fd;
    struct flock lock;
    int ret;

    // Open the file
    fd = open(filename, O_RDWR);
    if (fd == -1) {
        perror("open");
        return C_ERR;
    }

    // Initialize the lock structure
    lock.l_type = F_WRLCK;
    lock.l_whence = SEEK_SET;
    lock.l_start = 0;
    lock.l_len = 0;

    // Acquire the lock
    ret = fcntl(fd, F_SETLK, &lock);
    if (ret == -1) {
        perror("fcntl");
        close(fd);
        return C_ERR;
    }

    // Keep the file descriptor open
    // so that the file will be locked as long as the process is up
    return C_OK;
}
```
This function opens the file specified by `filename` in read-write mode, and then acquires an exclusive lock on the file using `fcntl` with the `F_SETLK` command. The lock is initialized with a `F_WRLCK` type, which means that it will be an exclusive lock, and it will be applied to the entire file. The `l_whence` field is set to `SEEK_SET`, which means that the lock will be applied to the beginning of the file. The `l_start` and `l_len` fields are set to 0, which means that the lock will be applied to the entire file.

If the lock is successfully acquired, the function returns `C_OK`, otherwise it returns `C_ERR` and logs an error message.

Note that the file descriptor is kept open so that the file will be locked as long as the process is up. This is because the file is updated in-place, and the lock is applied to the file descriptor, so if the file descriptor is closed, the lock will be released.

